WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.911
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.911 --> 00:00:08.095
<v Tutor>It's Monday the second of November, 128 days until the written exam, and this is the week four primer.

3
00:00:08.095 --> 00:00:11.131
<v Tutor>The theme this week is cardiac intensive care.

4
00:00:11.131 --> 00:00:12.761
<v Tutor>Why does that matter?

5
00:00:12.761 --> 00:00:15.628
<v Tutor>Because the College keeps coming back here.

6
00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:22.768
<v Tutor>Electrocardiogram interpretation appears in seventeen past College questions in our bank, including both 2026 sittings.

7
00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:30.694
<v Tutor>Cardiac arrest, advanced life support and post-cardiac-surgery care all sit near the top of the syllabus by number of past questions.

8
00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:32.549
<v Tutor>Here's the running order.

9
00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:43.680
<v Tutor>First, the shape of the week: how the College asks cardiac questions, the frameworks that score, and the core conditions as Oh's teaches them, with real numbers and the governing guidelines.

10
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.819
<v Tutor>Second, two landmark temperature trials, TTM and TTM2, and the story of how cooling after cardiac arrest rose and fell.

11
00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:56.497
<v Tutor>Third, exam practice on neuroprognostication after cardiac arrest, a real question from 2021.

12
00:00:56.497 --> 00:01:02.400
<v Tutor>Then a syllabus round-up of six more cardiac topics, and one thing to do before the next episode.

13
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.632
<v Tutor>Picture a Tuesday night.

14
00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:10.786
<v Tutor>A 58-year-old man is wheeled in from the cath lab after an out-of-hospital arrest.

15
00:01:10.786 --> 00:01:17.486
<v Tutor>He has a pulse, a stent in his left anterior descending artery, a noradrenaline infusion, and no eye opening.

16
00:01:17.486 --> 00:01:28.309
<v Tutor>Within the next hour you will be asked about his blood pressure target, his oxygen target, his temperature, whether he needs a CT, and, sooner than you'd like, what his chances are.

17
00:01:28.309 --> 00:01:32.661
<v Tutor>Almost every one of those questions has appeared in a College paper.

18
00:01:32.661 --> 00:01:35.925
<v Tutor>Let's start with how the College asks this block.

19
00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:37.757
<v Tutor>There are four patterns.

20
00:01:37.757 --> 00:01:39.647
<v Tutor>First, the data question.

21
00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:48.236
<v Tutor>An electrocardiogram is printed in the booklet and you are asked to list the abnormalities, give the likely causes, and name the complication.

22
00:01:48.236 --> 00:01:53.733
<v Tutor>That format has appeared seventeen times in our bank, most recently in both 2026 papers.

23
00:01:53.733 --> 00:01:58.085
<v Tutor>It is cheap marks if you are systematic and expensive if you aren't.

24
00:01:58.085 --> 00:02:00.433
<v Tutor>Second, the post-arrest question.

25
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:06.560
<v Tutor>Prognostication after cardiac arrest has been asked five times, in 2011, 2018, 2021, 2023 and 2024.

26
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.657
<v Tutor>The verbs vary: list the tests, discuss their limitations, outline your approach.

27
00:02:11.657 --> 00:02:14.520
<v Tutor>Third, the arrhythmia management question.

28
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:25.462
<v Tutor>Atrial fibrillation in the critically ill has been asked four times, in 2012, 2021, 2024 and 2025, usually as outline your approach to assessment and management.

29
00:02:25.462 --> 00:02:31.129
<v Tutor>Fourth, the post-cardiac-surgery question: the shocked patient back from theatre.

30
00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:38.325
<v Tutor>Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction has been asked three times, in 2019 and twice in 2025.

31
00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:45.266
<v Tutor>Older papers also loved troubleshooting the temporary pacemaker, six questions between 2008 and 2013.

32
00:02:45.266 --> 00:02:52.843
<v Tutor>So the week has a clear spine: the rhythm, the arrest, the brain after the arrest, and the heart after surgery.

33
00:02:52.843 --> 00:03:06.980
<v Tutor>Our anchor reading is Oh's Intensive Care Manual, ninth edition: chapter 30 on cardiopulmonary resuscitation, chapter 31 on arrhythmias, chapter 32 on pacing, and chapter 73 on postoperative cardiac intensive care.

34
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.880
<v Tutor>Start with the electrocardiogram.

35
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.662
<v Tutor>The framework examiners reward is boring and it works.

36
00:03:13.662 --> 00:03:14.454
<v Tutor>Rate.

37
00:03:14.454 --> 00:03:15.369
<v Tutor>Rhythm.

38
00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:16.162
<v Tutor>Axis.

39
00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:19.089
<v Tutor>Intervals: PR, QRS and the corrected QT.

40
00:03:19.089 --> 00:03:22.931
<v Tutor>Then morphology: P waves, QRS, ST segments and T waves.

41
00:03:22.931 --> 00:03:27.018
<v Tutor>Then, and this is where candidates drop marks, the so what.

42
00:03:27.018 --> 00:03:34.031
<v Tutor>Every abnormality you name should be followed by its likely cause in this patient, and the risk it carries.

43
00:03:34.031 --> 00:03:36.348
<v Tutor>Write it that way in the exam.

44
00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:49.156
<v Tutor>If a question gives five percent for the abnormalities and twenty percent for the aetiologies, as the 2021 second sitting did in question 23, the marks are in the causes, not in a beautiful description.

45
00:03:49.156 --> 00:03:52.144
<v Tutor>The high-yield patterns are a short list.

46
00:03:52.144 --> 00:03:58.730
<v Tutor>Hyperkalaemia: peaked T waves, then flattened P waves, then a broad QRS heading towards a sine wave.

47
00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:05.927
<v Tutor>Brugada pattern: coved ST elevation of at least two millimetres in V1 and V2, sloping into an inverted T wave.

48
00:04:05.927 --> 00:04:10.806
<v Tutor>Wolff-Parkinson-White: short PR under 120 milliseconds and a delta wave.

49
00:04:10.806 --> 00:04:16.660
<v Tutor>Long QT: a corrected QT above 500 milliseconds carries real risk of torsades de pointes.

50
00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:26.540
<v Tutor>ST elevation in lead a V R with widespread ST depression: think left main or severe three-vessel disease, or a global subendocardial ischaemia from shock.

51
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:35.201
<v Tutor>Then Mobitz type two block and complete heart block, pericarditis, the Osborn wave of hypothermia, and the right heart strain pattern of pulmonary embolism.

52
00:04:35.201 --> 00:04:39.080
<v Tutor>The classic higher-order question is the broad complex tachycardia.

53
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.777
<v Tutor>Here's your first chance to recall.

54
00:04:41.777 --> 00:04:46.424
<v Tutor>You are looking at a regular broad complex tachycardia at 200 beats per minute.

55
00:04:46.424 --> 00:04:54.971
<v Tutor>Name three features on the electrocardiogram that make ventricular tachycardia more likely than a supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction.

56
00:04:54.971 --> 00:04:56.360
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

57
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.871
<v Tutor>Here are the ones that score.

58
00:05:03.871 --> 00:05:07.806
<v Tutor>AV dissociation, with P waves marching through independently.

59
00:05:07.806 --> 00:05:12.883
<v Tutor>Capture beats and fusion beats, which are the visible proof of that dissociation.

60
00:05:12.883 --> 00:05:23.493
<v Tutor>No RS complex in any precordial lead, or an R to S interval longer than 100 milliseconds in any precordial lead: those are the first two steps of the Brugada algorithm from 1991.

61
00:05:23.493 --> 00:05:26.972
<v Tutor>Precordial concordance, all positive or all negative.

62
00:05:26.972 --> 00:05:29.197
<v Tutor>And an extreme, northwest axis.

63
00:05:29.197 --> 00:05:38.266
<v Tutor>Add the bedside rule: in an older patient with structural heart disease, a broad complex tachycardia is ventricular tachycardia until proven otherwise.

64
00:05:38.266 --> 00:05:39.749
<v Tutor>Treat it that way.

65
00:05:39.749 --> 00:05:45.853
<v Tutor>One more pairing worth a mark on its own: atrial fibrillation with a pre-excited accessory pathway.

66
00:05:45.853 --> 00:05:49.731
<v Tutor>Irregular, very fast, with QRS complexes that vary in width.

67
00:05:49.731 --> 00:05:51.956
<v Tutor>Never give an AV nodal blocker.

68
00:05:51.956 --> 00:05:56.348
<v Tutor>Not adenosine, not verapamil, not digoxin, and be wary of amiodarone.

69
00:05:56.348 --> 00:06:02.337
<v Tutor>Block the node and you push conduction down the pathway, and ventricular fibrillation can follow.

70
00:06:02.337 --> 00:06:08.840
<v Tutor>The answer is synchronised DC cardioversion if unstable, or a class one drug such as flecainide if stable.

71
00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.682
<v Tutor>Now cardiac arrest.

72
00:06:10.682 --> 00:06:16.182
<v Tutor>A short story first, because it explains why compressions matter more than anything else we do.

73
00:06:16.182 --> 00:06:22.589
<v Tutor>In the 1950s, at Johns Hopkins, an electrical engineer called William Kouwenhoven was working on defibrillation.

74
00:06:22.589 --> 00:06:24.084
<v Tutor>He was not a doctor.

75
00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:29.637
<v Tutor>A graduate student on his team, Guy Knickerbocker, noticed something odd in the laboratory dogs.

76
00:06:29.637 --> 00:06:33.375
<v Tutor>When the chest was compressed, femoral arterial pressure rose.

77
00:06:33.375 --> 00:06:35.618
<v Tutor>Pushing on the chest made a pulse.

78
00:06:35.618 --> 00:06:43.093
<v Tutor>With the surgeon James Jude, they took it to patients, and in 1960 the three of them published Closed Chest Cardiac Massage in JAMA.

79
00:06:43.093 --> 00:06:46.884
<v Tutor>Their series reported fourteen survivors among twenty patients.

80
00:06:46.884 --> 00:06:51.316
<v Tutor>Before that, resuscitation outside theatre largely meant opening the chest.

81
00:06:51.316 --> 00:06:55.268
<v Tutor>Within a few years, closed chest compression was taught worldwide.

82
00:06:55.268 --> 00:07:01.835
<v Tutor>That's the exam point: every modern guideline still puts high-quality compressions with minimal interruption first.

83
00:07:01.835 --> 00:07:06.694
<v Tutor>Here are the numbers from Oh's chapter 30 and the current resuscitation guidelines.

84
00:07:06.694 --> 00:07:11.607
<v Tutor>Compressions at 100 to 120 a minute, five to six centimetres deep, with full recoil.

85
00:07:11.607 --> 00:07:17.000
<v Tutor>Thirty compressions to two breaths until the airway is secured, then continuous compressions.

86
00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.729
<v Tutor>Shockable rhythm: shock, then two minutes of compressions before the rhythm check.

87
00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:28.216
<v Tutor>Adrenaline one milligram after the third shock, then every three to five minutes.

88
00:07:28.216 --> 00:07:33.888
<v Tutor>Amiodarone 300 milligrams after the third shock, and 150 milligrams after the fifth.

89
00:07:33.888 --> 00:07:39.128
<v Tutor>Non-shockable rhythm: adrenaline one milligram as soon as access is obtained.

90
00:07:39.128 --> 00:07:47.081
<v Tutor>Use waveform capnography to confirm tube position, judge compression quality, and spot return of spontaneous circulation.

91
00:07:47.081 --> 00:07:57.500
<v Tutor>Hunt the reversible causes: hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hyperkalaemia and other metabolic causes, hypothermia, tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins and thrombosis.

92
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.756
<v Tutor>Two adjuncts to compressions have been asked about, mechanical devices in 2015 and 2024.

93
00:08:03.756 --> 00:08:11.321
<v Tutor>Automated chest compression devices deliver consistent compressions, but the large trials found no survival benefit.

94
00:08:11.321 --> 00:08:18.153
<v Tutor>LINC, in 2014, randomised about 2,600 patients and found four-hour survival of 23.6 versus 23.7 percent.

95
00:08:18.153 --> 00:08:27.486
<v Tutor>PARAMEDIC, in 2015, randomised about 4,500 patients, and 30-day survival was 6 percent with the device versus 7 percent with manual compressions.

96
00:08:27.486 --> 00:08:29.743
<v Tutor>So the device is not routine.

97
00:08:29.743 --> 00:08:39.626
<v Tutor>Its place is where manual compressions are unsafe or impossible: in a moving ambulance, in the cath lab during PCI, and as a bridge to extracorporeal CPR.

98
00:08:39.626 --> 00:08:46.702
<v Tutor>The limitations are worth a line too: the pause to apply it, and injuries such as rib and sternal fractures.

99
00:08:46.702 --> 00:08:52.436
<v Tutor>Extracorporeal CPR, venoarterial ECMO started during the arrest, is the other adjunct.

100
00:08:52.436 --> 00:08:54.267
<v Tutor>The evidence is mixed.

101
00:08:54.267 --> 00:09:04.820
<v Tutor>The small ARREST trial in Minneapolis, with 30 patients in refractory ventricular fibrillation, was stopped early when survival to discharge was 43 versus 7 percent.

102
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:20.104
<v Tutor>The Prague trial of 256 patients found no significant difference in its primary outcome, and INCEPTION, a pragmatic Dutch multicentre trial of 134 patients, found 30-day survival with good neurology of 20 versus 16 percent, not significant.

103
00:09:20.104 --> 00:09:26.560
<v Tutor>The message for the exam: ECPR works only in a system built for it, with tight selection criteria.

104
00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:33.320
<v Tutor>Young, witnessed, shockable rhythm, bystander CPR, and cannulation within about 60 minutes of collapse.

105
00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:38.712
<v Tutor>Cardiac arrest after cardiac surgery is different, and it's on this week's reading list.

106
00:09:38.712 --> 00:09:43.340
<v Tutor>The guidance from the European cardiothoracic surgeons in 2017 changed the order.

107
00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:50.100
<v Tutor>For witnessed ventricular fibrillation in the unit, give up to three quick successive shocks before starting compressions.

108
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.832
<v Tutor>For asystole or extreme bradycardia, connect the epicardial wires and pace at once.

109
00:09:54.832 --> 00:10:02.684
<v Tutor>If there's no output, prepare for emergency resternotomy within five minutes, because tamponade and bleeding are common and correctable causes.

110
00:10:02.684 --> 00:10:09.600
<v Tutor>Full-dose adrenaline is avoided in this setting because of the risk of rebound hypertension and bleeding at the suture lines.

111
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.489
<v Tutor>Next, the post-resuscitation hour.

112
00:10:12.489 --> 00:10:27.009
<v Tutor>The governing guideline is the European Resuscitation Council and European Society of Intensive Care Medicine guidelines on post-resuscitation care, the 2025 edition, published in Intensive Care Medicine with Jerry Nolan as first author.

113
00:10:27.009 --> 00:10:29.557
<v Tutor>ANZCOR guidance is closely aligned.

114
00:10:29.557 --> 00:10:31.572
<v Tutor>Here's your second recall.

115
00:10:31.572 --> 00:10:39.099
<v Tutor>Give me the post-arrest targets for mean arterial pressure, oxygen saturation, arterial carbon dioxide and temperature.

116
00:10:39.099 --> 00:10:40.640
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

117
00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:54.768
<v Tutor>Mean arterial pressure above 60 to 65 millimetres of mercury, with systolic above 100, guided by an arterial line and early echocardiography.

118
00:10:54.768 --> 00:11:06.659
<v Tutor>Start with 100 percent oxygen, then titrate to saturations of 94 to 98 percent, or an arterial oxygen tension of about 75 to 100 millimetres of mercury, avoiding both hypoxaemia and hyperoxaemia.

119
00:11:06.659 --> 00:11:14.508
<v Tutor>Normocapnia: an arterial carbon dioxide of 35 to 45 millimetres of mercury, and watch for hypocapnia when the patient is cold.

120
00:11:14.508 --> 00:11:19.722
<v Tutor>Temperature: the guideline no longer talks about targeted temperature management.

121
00:11:19.722 --> 00:11:22.241
<v Tutor>It talks about temperature control.

122
00:11:22.241 --> 00:11:34.308
<v Tutor>Actively prevent fever, keeping core temperature at 37.5 degrees or below, through the first days in patients who remain comatose, and don't actively rewarm a patient who arrives mildly hypothermic.

123
00:11:34.308 --> 00:11:37.998
<v Tutor>Several 2025 changes are worth a sentence in an answer.

124
00:11:37.998 --> 00:11:41.747
<v Tutor>If there's ST elevation, go to the cath lab immediately.

125
00:11:41.747 --> 00:11:52.818
<v Tutor>If there's no ST elevation after an out-of-hospital arrest, delay angiography unless acute coronary occlusion is strongly suspected; that rests on trials such as COACT and TOMAHAWK.

126
00:11:52.818 --> 00:11:59.320
<v Tutor>If the cause is unclear, consider a whole-body CT from head to pelvis, including a pulmonary angiogram.

127
00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:08.028
<v Tutor>Use EEG to diagnose seizures, treat them with levetiracetam or sodium valproate alongside sedation, and don't give seizure prophylaxis.

128
00:12:08.028 --> 00:12:14.707
<v Tutor>Use short-acting sedation, avoid routine neuromuscular blockade, and plan rehabilitation from the start.

129
00:12:14.707 --> 00:12:32.300
<v Tutor>Don't forget the ordinary things that earn marks in a list: standard glucose control, avoiding hypoglycaemia, early echo for the stunned myocardium, which usually recovers over 48 to 72 hours, and lung-protective ventilation at six to eight millilitres per kilogram of ideal body weight.

130
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:35.682
<v Tutor>One more scenario from this week's reading list.

131
00:12:35.682 --> 00:12:40.794
<v Tutor>A 35-year-old collapses while running and is resuscitated from ventricular fibrillation.

132
00:12:40.794 --> 00:12:42.178
<v Tutor>Here's the recall.

133
00:12:42.178 --> 00:12:47.876
<v Tutor>List four causes of sudden cardiac death in a young adult, and the electrocardiogram clue for each.

134
00:12:47.876 --> 00:12:49.260
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

135
00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.266
<v Tutor>Think structural, then electrical.

136
00:12:57.266 --> 00:13:20.597
<v Tutor>Structural: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, with voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy and deep T wave inversion; arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, with T wave inversion in V1 to V3 and sometimes an epsilon wave; myocarditis, with ST changes and arrhythmia after a viral illness; and anomalous coronary arteries, which may show nothing at rest.

137
00:13:20.597 --> 00:13:38.777
<v Tutor>Electrical: long QT syndrome, with a corrected QT above 500 milliseconds; Brugada syndrome, with coved ST elevation in V1 and V2; Wolff-Parkinson-White, with a short PR and a delta wave; and catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, which usually has a normal resting ECG.

138
00:13:38.777 --> 00:13:47.712
<v Tutor>Then the acquired causes that the examiner also accepts: drugs such as cocaine, electrolyte disturbance, and premature coronary disease.

139
00:13:47.712 --> 00:13:53.111
<v Tutor>The 2025 post-resuscitation guideline adds a new section on unexplained arrest.

140
00:13:53.111 --> 00:14:04.900
<v Tutor>If no cause is found, the work-up extends to cardiac MRI, provocation testing and genetic testing, with screening of first-degree relatives, because these conditions run in families.

141
00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:14.458
<v Tutor>This is also where the cardiac block meets acid-base, which has been your weakest area in the marked answers so far, averaging around five out of ten.

142
00:14:14.458 --> 00:14:17.820
<v Tutor>Expect a post-arrest blood gas in a data question.

143
00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:25.355
<v Tutor>A typical one: pH 7.05, arterial carbon dioxide 60 millimetres of mercury, bicarbonate 16 millimoles per litre, lactate 9.

144
00:14:25.355 --> 00:14:27.036
<v Tutor>Do the sums out loud.

145
00:14:27.036 --> 00:14:35.324
<v Tutor>Anion gap, the College way, without potassium: sodium minus chloride plus bicarbonate, with a normal of 12, and correct it for albumin.

146
00:14:35.324 --> 00:14:39.324
<v Tutor>Then the delta ratio, to look for a second metabolic process.

147
00:14:39.324 --> 00:14:46.279
<v Tutor>Then the expected carbon dioxide for the metabolic acidosis, 1.5 times the bicarbonate plus 8, which here is 32.

148
00:14:46.279 --> 00:14:53.061
<v Tutor>A measured 60 means a coexisting respiratory acidosis from inadequate ventilation during or after the arrest.

149
00:14:53.061 --> 00:15:02.972
<v Tutor>The lactate should fall by at least half within a few hours of restoring perfusion; if it doesn't, look for ongoing low output, mesenteric ischaemia, or a seizure.

150
00:15:02.972 --> 00:15:06.914
<v Tutor>Write the formula, then the number, then the interpretation.

151
00:15:06.914 --> 00:15:09.580
<v Tutor>That's where the marks went last time.

152
00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:15.438
<v Tutor>Now arrhythmias in the critically ill, and the most common one: atrial fibrillation.

153
00:15:15.438 --> 00:15:26.354
<v Tutor>In the general ICU, new atrial fibrillation is usually the illness talking: catecholamines, atrial stretch from fluid, inflammation, electrolyte loss, a line tip in the atrium.

154
00:15:26.354 --> 00:15:34.422
<v Tutor>After cardiac surgery it is even more common, affecting roughly a quarter to a third of patients, peaking on days two and three.

155
00:15:34.422 --> 00:15:37.151
<v Tutor>The answer that scores has two halves.

156
00:15:37.151 --> 00:15:52.160
<v Tutor>Assessment: is the patient haemodynamically unstable; when did it start; a 12-lead ECG; potassium, magnesium, troponin and thyroid function; an echo for ventricular function, valves and atrial size; and the stroke risk against the bleeding risk.

157
00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:59.517
<v Tutor>The 2024 European Society of Cardiology guideline now uses CHA two DS two VA, which drops female sex from the score.

158
00:15:59.517 --> 00:16:01.712
<v Tutor>Management: treat the driver.

159
00:16:01.712 --> 00:16:06.399
<v Tutor>Correct potassium to about 4.5 millimoles per litre and give magnesium.

160
00:16:06.399 --> 00:16:08.890
<v Tutor>Wean catecholamines where you can.

161
00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:11.916
<v Tutor>If unstable, synchronised DC cardioversion.

162
00:16:11.916 --> 00:16:20.340
<v Tutor>For atrial fibrillation, the European resuscitation guidance suggests starting at maximum defibrillator output rather than escalating.

163
00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:29.884
<v Tutor>Flutter and regular narrow complex tachycardias often respond to 70 to 120 joules, and monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a pulse to 120 to 150.

164
00:16:29.884 --> 00:16:39.782
<v Tutor>If stable, rate control is usually first: a beta-blocker if blood pressure allows, amiodarone if it doesn't, digoxin as an add-on in poor ventricular function.

165
00:16:39.782 --> 00:16:50.864
<v Tutor>After cardiac surgery, a 2016 trial of 523 patients from the Cardiothoracic Surgical Trials Network found rate control and rhythm control gave the same number of days in hospital.

166
00:16:50.864 --> 00:16:54.776
<v Tutor>So either strategy is defensible; say why you chose yours.

167
00:16:54.776 --> 00:16:58.036
<v Tutor>Torsades de pointes is the other rhythm to own.

168
00:16:58.036 --> 00:17:10.600
<v Tutor>Magnesium two grams intravenously, stop every QT-prolonging drug, correct potassium, and if it recurs, increase the heart rate with isoprenaline or overdrive pacing, because a faster rate shortens the QT.

169
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.954
<v Tutor>Pacing, from Oh's chapter 32.

170
00:17:12.954 --> 00:17:18.395
<v Tutor>Know the three-letter code: the chamber paced, the chamber sensed, and the response to sensing.

171
00:17:18.395 --> 00:17:22.673
<v Tutor>So D D D paces and senses both chambers, and either inhibits or triggers.

172
00:17:22.673 --> 00:17:26.476
<v Tutor>V O O paces the ventricle asynchronously and ignores everything.

173
00:17:26.476 --> 00:17:29.434
<v Tutor>The troubleshooting question has three failures.

174
00:17:29.434 --> 00:17:40.685
<v Tutor>Failure to pace, where there's no pacing spike: check the connections, the battery and the box, and look for oversensing, where the pacemaker mistakes noise or T waves for native beats and inhibits itself.

175
00:17:40.685 --> 00:17:49.136
<v Tutor>Failure to capture, where there's a spike with no complex after it: increase the output, check the wires, and correct potassium, acidosis and ischaemia.

176
00:17:49.136 --> 00:17:56.161
<v Tutor>Failure to sense, where it fires on top of native beats: increase the sensitivity, which means lowering the millivolt number.

177
00:17:56.161 --> 00:18:01.495
<v Tutor>Set output at two to three times the capture threshold, and keep transcutaneous pacing ready.

178
00:18:01.495 --> 00:18:07.200
<v Tutor>If you're unsure in an emergency, switch to an asynchronous mode at a high output and fix the cause.

179
00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:08.790
<v Tutor>Quick recall.

180
00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.981
<v Tutor>Day one after valve surgery, paced D D D through epicardial wires.

181
00:18:12.981 --> 00:18:19.269
<v Tutor>On the monitor you see pacing spikes with no QRS complex after them, and the blood pressure is falling.

182
00:18:19.269 --> 00:18:23.007
<v Tutor>What's the failure, and what are your first three actions?

183
00:18:23.007 --> 00:18:24.480
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

184
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.812
<v Tutor>That's failure to capture.

185
00:18:31.812 --> 00:18:39.028
<v Tutor>First, check the patient and support the circulation, and have transcutaneous pacing or an isoprenaline infusion ready.

186
00:18:39.028 --> 00:18:44.199
<v Tutor>Second, increase the output to maximum and check every connection from wire to box.

187
00:18:44.199 --> 00:18:53.518
<v Tutor>Third, look for the reasons the threshold has risen: lead displacement, oedema around the wire, hyperkalaemia, acidosis, ischaemia, or antiarrhythmic drugs.

188
00:18:53.518 --> 00:18:58.120
<v Tutor>Epicardial thresholds rise over days, which is why they're checked daily.

189
00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.368
<v Tutor>Where do candidates lose marks in this block?

190
00:19:01.368 --> 00:19:06.257
<v Tutor>The College's comment on the 2019 question on dynamic outflow obstruction is blunt.

191
00:19:06.257 --> 00:19:11.845
<v Tutor>Candidates who did poorly recommended dangerous therapies, including inotropes and vasodilators.

192
00:19:11.845 --> 00:19:16.627
<v Tutor>In a heart that obstructs when it empties hard and fast, inotropes make it worse.

193
00:19:16.627 --> 00:19:24.472
<v Tutor>For prognostication, the commonest failure is listing tests without their limitations, which is exactly what that 2021 question asked for.

194
00:19:24.472 --> 00:19:30.275
<v Tutor>And for electrocardiograms, the commonest failure is a description with no cause and no consequence.

195
00:19:30.275 --> 00:19:32.102
<v Tutor>Three numbers to remember.

196
00:19:32.102 --> 00:19:36.132
<v Tutor>37.5 degrees: the ceiling for temperature in the comatose survivor.

197
00:19:36.132 --> 00:19:39.839
<v Tutor>72 hours: the earliest point to draw a prognostic conclusion.

198
00:19:39.839 --> 00:19:45.320
<v Tutor>And five minutes: the time to resternotomy when a post-cardiac-surgery arrest doesn't respond.

199
00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:48.257
<v Tutor>Here's a question.

200
00:19:48.257 --> 00:19:57.005
<v Tutor>If cooling the brain after cardiac arrest was the standard of care for a decade, why do the guidelines now talk only about preventing fever?

201
00:19:57.005 --> 00:20:00.138
<v Tutor>The idea of cold as a brain protector is old.

202
00:20:00.138 --> 00:20:06.167
<v Tutor>Cardiac surgeons used hypothermia in the 1950s to protect the brain during circulatory arrest.

203
00:20:06.167 --> 00:20:09.654
<v Tutor>But for decades it didn't cross into resuscitation.

204
00:20:09.654 --> 00:20:15.979
<v Tutor>Then, in February 2002, the New England Journal of Medicine published two trials in the same issue.

205
00:20:15.979 --> 00:20:19.289
<v Tutor>One, from Melbourne, was led by Stephen Bernard.

206
00:20:19.289 --> 00:20:24.550
<v Tutor>The other was the European Hypothermia After Cardiac Arrest trial, known as HACA.

207
00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:31.998
<v Tutor>HACA randomised 275 patients after ventricular fibrillation arrest to 32 to 34 degrees for 24 hours, or standard care.

208
00:20:31.998 --> 00:20:38.795
<v Tutor>Favourable neurological outcome at six months was 55 versus 39 percent, and mortality 41 versus 55 percent.

209
00:20:38.795 --> 00:20:44.351
<v Tutor>ILCOR endorsed therapeutic hypothermia the following year, and cooling became routine.

210
00:20:44.351 --> 00:20:46.479
<v Tutor>But look at the control arm.

211
00:20:46.479 --> 00:20:51.799
<v Tutor>In HACA, normothermia wasn't controlled, and many control patients became febrile.

212
00:20:51.799 --> 00:20:55.405
<v Tutor>So was the benefit from cold, or from avoiding fever?

213
00:20:55.405 --> 00:21:03.680
<v Tutor>Meanwhile the practice spread far beyond the trial populations: to asystole, to in-hospital arrest, to patients with long downtimes.

214
00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:07.966
<v Tutor>A therapy proven in a narrow group had become routine for everyone.

215
00:21:07.966 --> 00:21:11.470
<v Tutor>Two Scandinavian-led trials set out to test it properly.

216
00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:23.460
<v Tutor>That's the pattern you'll see again and again this year, from tight glucose control to early goal-directed therapy: a striking early trial, rapid adoption, then a larger pragmatic trial that finds no difference.

217
00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:28.097
<v Tutor>TTM, published in 2013 by Niklas Nielsen and colleagues.

218
00:21:28.097 --> 00:21:36.175
<v Tutor>950 unconscious adults after out-of-hospital arrest of presumed cardiac cause, in 36 ICUs in Europe and Australia.

219
00:21:36.175 --> 00:21:39.221
<v Tutor>Intervention: 33 degrees for 28 hours.

220
00:21:39.221 --> 00:21:42.134
<v Tutor>Comparator: 36 degrees for 28 hours.

221
00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:46.967
<v Tutor>Both arms were rewarmed slowly and fever was avoided to 72 hours.

222
00:21:46.967 --> 00:21:59.282
<v Tutor>The primary outcome, all-cause mortality, was 50 percent at 33 degrees versus 48 percent at 36, with a hazard ratio of 1.06, and a 95 percent confidence interval of 0.89 to 1.28.

223
00:21:59.282 --> 00:22:04.778
<v Tutor>Death or poor neurological function at six months was 54 versus 52 percent.

224
00:22:04.778 --> 00:22:19.212
<v Tutor>The main criticisms: the confidence interval was wide, unwitnessed arrests with asystole were excluded, so the sickest brains were under-represented, and some argued that 36 degrees was still a form of cooling.

225
00:22:19.212 --> 00:22:24.840
<v Tutor>What it changed: many units moved to 36 degrees, which was easier to deliver.

226
00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:27.488
<v Tutor>There's a nuance worth one line.

227
00:22:27.488 --> 00:22:38.082
<v Tutor>TTM did not test cooling against nothing; both arms received a carefully controlled temperature, and that is exactly what made the next trial necessary.

228
00:22:38.082 --> 00:22:45.100
<v Tutor>How to quote it: in TTM, 33 degrees was no better than 36, with mortality of 50 versus 48 percent.

229
00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:49.886
<v Tutor>TTM2, published in 2021 by Josef Dankiewicz and colleagues.

230
00:22:49.886 --> 00:22:57.480
<v Tutor>1,900 patients were randomised in 61 centres in 14 countries, with 1,861 in the intention-to-treat analysis.

231
00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:04.419
<v Tutor>Adults after out-of-hospital arrest of presumed cardiac or unknown cause, with any initial rhythm.

232
00:23:04.419 --> 00:23:08.478
<v Tutor>Intervention: 33 degrees for 28 hours, then rewarming.

233
00:23:08.478 --> 00:23:16.333
<v Tutor>Comparator: normothermia, aiming for 37.5 degrees or below, with a device used only if temperature reached 37.8.

234
00:23:16.333 --> 00:23:24.385
<v Tutor>Six-month mortality was 50 versus 48 percent, a relative risk of 1.04, 95 percent confidence interval 0.94 to 1.14.

235
00:23:24.385 --> 00:23:28.313
<v Tutor>Poor functional outcome was 55 percent in both arms.

236
00:23:28.313 --> 00:23:35.317
<v Tutor>The harm: arrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise in 24 versus 16 percent in the hypothermia group.

237
00:23:35.317 --> 00:23:50.243
<v Tutor>Criticisms: it was open-label, follow-up was disrupted by COVID, about a fifth of patients were co-enrolled in the TAME carbon dioxide trial, and it can't exclude benefit in a subgroup with severe but salvageable injury.

238
00:23:50.243 --> 00:24:02.616
<v Tutor>What it changed: the 2022 ERC and ESICM temperature guideline, and then the 2025 post-resuscitation guideline, replaced targeted temperature management with active fever prevention.

239
00:24:02.616 --> 00:24:06.543
<v Tutor>What does fever prevention look like at the bedside?

240
00:24:06.543 --> 00:24:08.180
<v Tutor>It's not passive.

241
00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:17.493
<v Tutor>Many patients still need a feedback-controlled surface or intravascular device, because fever after cardiac arrest is common in the first 72 hours.

242
00:24:17.493 --> 00:24:32.872
<v Tutor>Shivering has to be managed, with a stepwise approach: skin counter-warming, paracetamol, magnesium, then deeper sedation or opioids, and neuromuscular blockade only as a last resort, because it masks seizures and confounds the neurological examination.

243
00:24:32.872 --> 00:24:42.358
<v Tutor>And remember the TTM2 harm: cooling to 33 degrees brought more arrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise, which matters in a patient who has just arrested.

244
00:24:42.358 --> 00:24:58.680
<v Tutor>How to quote it in an SAQ: TTM2 found no mortality benefit from 33 degrees over fever prevention, 50 versus 48 percent at six months, with more haemodynamically significant arrhythmia, so I would actively prevent fever above 37.5 degrees rather than induce hypothermia.

245
00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:02.161
<v Tutor>Now let's go to the exam room.

246
00:25:02.161 --> 00:25:05.556
<v Tutor>This is from the 2021 first sitting, question sixteen.

247
00:25:05.556 --> 00:25:10.320
<v Tutor>Neuroprognostication after cardiac arrest has been asked five times since 2011.

248
00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:14.155
<v Examiner>Before you start, a word on reading the question.

249
00:25:14.155 --> 00:25:17.501
<v Examiner>The verb is discuss, and the object is their limitations.

250
00:25:17.501 --> 00:25:19.921
<v Examiner>Half the marks are for the limitations.

251
00:25:19.921 --> 00:25:21.260
<v Examiner>I'll read it once.

252
00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:24.115
<v Examiner>Here is the question, as written.

253
00:25:24.115 --> 00:25:31.540
<v Examiner>List the clinical signs and tests used for neuro-prognostication after cardiac arrest and discuss their limitations.

254
00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:32.777
<v Candidate>Okay.

255
00:25:32.777 --> 00:25:40.653
<v Candidate>I'll group them into four categories: clinical examination, electrophysiology, biomarkers and imaging, with the limitation of each.

256
00:25:40.653 --> 00:25:42.637
<v Candidate>Clinical examination first.

257
00:25:42.637 --> 00:25:46.773
<v Candidate>Bilaterally absent pupillary light reflexes at 72 hours or later.

258
00:25:46.773 --> 00:25:48.587
<v Candidate>Absent corneal reflexes.

259
00:25:48.587 --> 00:25:51.420
<v Candidate>A motor response that's extensor or worse.

260
00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:54.537
<v Candidate>And status myoclonus within the first 72 hours.

261
00:25:54.537 --> 00:26:01.847
<v Candidate>The limitation of all clinical signs is confounding by sedatives, opioids and neuromuscular blockers, and by hypothermia.

262
00:26:01.847 --> 00:26:10.687
<v Candidate>The motor response in particular has a high false positive rate, so it's used to select patients for prognostication rather than to predict outcome.

263
00:26:10.687 --> 00:26:17.090
<v Candidate>Myoclonus can be subtle, it's interpreter dependent, and some patients with Lance-Adams syndrome do well.

264
00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:18.563
<v Candidate>Electrophysiology.

265
00:26:18.563 --> 00:26:23.097
<v Candidate>Somatosensory evoked potentials: a bilaterally absent N twenty response.

266
00:26:23.097 --> 00:26:31.880
<v Candidate>It's quite specific, but the limitation is that it's insensitive, it can be degraded by electrical interference in the ICU, and it needs expertise.

267
00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:36.697
<v Candidate>EEG: highly malignant patterns, a suppressed background or burst suppression.

268
00:26:36.697 --> 00:26:43.270
<v Candidate>The limitation is that sedation can produce the same pattern, and the interpretation varies between readers.

269
00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:44.347
<v Candidate>Biomarkers.

270
00:26:44.347 --> 00:26:46.160
<v Candidate>Neuron-specific enolase.

271
00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:48.843
<v Candidate>A high level suggests a poor outcome.

272
00:26:48.843 --> 00:26:52.237
<v Candidate>I'd use a threshold of about 33 micrograms per litre.

273
00:26:52.237 --> 00:26:56.687
<v Candidate>The limitation is haemolysis, which falsely raises it, and some tumours.

274
00:26:56.687 --> 00:26:57.577
<v Candidate>Imaging.

275
00:26:57.577 --> 00:27:03.363
<v Candidate>CT showing loss of grey-white differentiation, and MRI showing widespread diffusion restriction.

276
00:27:03.363 --> 00:27:09.260
<v Candidate>CT may be normal if it's done too early, and there's no agreed threshold for the grey-white ratio.

277
00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:10.912
<v Examiner>Good structure.

278
00:27:10.912 --> 00:27:11.864
<v Examiner>Two things.

279
00:27:11.864 --> 00:27:15.520
<v Examiner>Where does 33 come from, and what does the current guideline use?

280
00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:23.711
<v Candidate>Thirty-three was the older American Academy of Neurology figure from 2006, from the era of hypothermia.

281
00:27:23.711 --> 00:27:33.620
<v Candidate>The ERC and ESICM threshold is above 60 micrograms per litre at 48 or 72 hours, and a rising level between 24 and 72 hours adds weight.

282
00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:35.007
<v Examiner>Correct.

283
00:27:35.007 --> 00:27:35.838
<v Examiner>Second.

284
00:27:35.838 --> 00:27:37.722
<v Examiner>You've given me the tests.

285
00:27:37.722 --> 00:27:39.384
<v Examiner>What frames their use?

286
00:27:39.384 --> 00:27:40.880
<v Examiner>When, and how many?

287
00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:42.112
<v Candidate>Right.

288
00:27:42.112 --> 00:27:44.150
<v Candidate>I should have opened with that.

289
00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:50.945
<v Candidate>First, exclude the confounders: sedation cleared, normothermia, no neuromuscular blockade, no major metabolic derangement.

290
00:27:50.945 --> 00:28:00.614
<v Candidate>Second, timing: prognostic conclusions are drawn in patients who remain unconscious at 72 hours or more after return of circulation, although some tests can be recorded earlier.

291
00:28:00.614 --> 00:28:02.235
<v Candidate>Third, it's multimodal.

292
00:28:02.235 --> 00:28:06.050
<v Candidate>A poor outcome is likely when two or more predictors are present.

293
00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:08.507
<v Candidate>No single test should drive withdrawal.

294
00:28:08.507 --> 00:28:13.315
<v Candidate>Fourth, the biggest limitation of the whole process is the self-fulfilling prophecy.

295
00:28:13.315 --> 00:28:21.260
<v Candidate>Many patients die after life-sustaining treatment is withdrawn, so the studies that validated these tests may have overestimated their accuracy.

296
00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:22.436
<v Examiner>Better.

297
00:28:22.436 --> 00:28:23.203
<v Examiner>One more.

298
00:28:23.203 --> 00:28:25.366
<v Examiner>You said the CT may be normal too early.

299
00:28:25.366 --> 00:28:27.440
<v Examiner>When does each imaging test work best?

300
00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:38.131
<v Candidate>CT is most useful within the first couple of days, and the guideline suggests repeating it at 72 to 96 hours if the first is normal and the patient is still unconscious.

301
00:28:38.131 --> 00:28:40.780
<v Candidate>MRI is best at about two to five days.

302
00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:44.580
<v Candidate>Done too late, the diffusion changes can pseudo-normalise.

303
00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:46.184
<v Examiner>Last probe.

304
00:28:46.184 --> 00:28:51.704
<v Examiner>A patient at day four has myoclonus, but the EEG background is continuous and reactive.

305
00:28:51.704 --> 00:28:53.040
<v Examiner>What do you do?

306
00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.470
<v Candidate>That's not status myoclonus with a malignant EEG.

307
00:28:56.470 --> 00:29:00.634
<v Candidate>It may be Lance-Adams syndrome, which is compatible with a good recovery.

308
00:29:00.634 --> 00:29:10.863
<v Candidate>I'd treat the myoclonus, with levetiracetam or valproate, and the guideline suggests a wake-up trial, reducing sedation over the following days, rather than drawing any prognostic conclusion.

309
00:29:10.863 --> 00:29:16.980
<v Candidate>I'd also make sure the family hears that uncertainty honestly, and that no decision is made on myoclonus alone.

310
00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:18.856
<v Examiner>Let me mark that.

311
00:29:18.856 --> 00:29:29.424
<v Examiner>The marks sit in four places: the right signs and tests, the right timing, the specific limitation of each one, and the framing of multimodal prognostication with confounders excluded.

312
00:29:29.424 --> 00:29:33.221
<v Examiner>You named all the major tests and gave a limitation for most.

313
00:29:33.221 --> 00:29:41.037
<v Examiner>You lost marks for the outdated enolase threshold, and for leaving confounders, timing and the self-fulfilling prophecy until I asked.

314
00:29:41.037 --> 00:29:43.569
<v Examiner>In the paper, nobody would have asked.

315
00:29:43.569 --> 00:29:46.211
<v Examiner>That's around six and a half out of ten.

316
00:29:46.211 --> 00:29:49.018
<v Examiner>With the framing at the top, it's an eight.

317
00:29:49.018 --> 00:29:51.439
<v Examiner>The one fix: open with when and how.

318
00:29:51.439 --> 00:29:54.907
<v Examiner>Confounders excluded, 72 hours, two or more predictors.

319
00:29:54.907 --> 00:29:58.264
<v Examiner>Then present each test with its limitation beside it.

320
00:29:58.264 --> 00:30:03.878
<v Examiner>A two-column table, test and limitation, is the fastest way to write this under time pressure.

321
00:30:03.878 --> 00:30:06.190
<v Examiner>And keep the limitations specific.

322
00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:13.400
<v Examiner>Confounded by sedation is worth a mark once; saying which test, which drug, and how long to wait is worth a mark each time.

323
00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:22.104
<v Tutor>Now the syllabus round-up: six more topics from this week's block, at overview level, each with what the examiner wants.

324
00:30:22.104 --> 00:30:27.410
<v Tutor>First, the patient after cardiac surgery: coronary grafts, valves and aortic surgery.

325
00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:28.780
<v Tutor>Oh's chapter 73.

326
00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:33.801
<v Tutor>This syllabus item has 25 past questions linked to it, so the framework matters.

327
00:30:33.801 --> 00:30:35.570
<v Tutor>Think in five problems.

328
00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:47.439
<v Tutor>Low cardiac output, usually defined as a cardiac index below 2.2 litres per minute per square metre with signs of hypoperfusion: check preload, rhythm, contractility and afterload, and look with echo.

329
00:30:47.439 --> 00:30:53.373
<v Tutor>Expect a dip in ventricular function at six to eight hours after bypass, which usually recovers.

330
00:30:53.373 --> 00:31:09.008
<v Tutor>Bleeding: correct temperature, calcium, protamine reversal of heparin, and coagulopathy guided by viscoelastic testing; commonly quoted re-exploration thresholds are more than 400 millilitres in the first hour, or more than 200 millilitres an hour for several hours.

331
00:31:09.008 --> 00:31:18.480
<v Tutor>Tamponade: it can be localised after cardiac surgery, so a clean transthoracic echo does not exclude it, and transoesophageal echo or reopening may be needed.

332
00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:27.839
<v Tutor>Vasoplegia: a low systemic vascular resistance with normal or high output, treated with noradrenaline and vasopressin, with methylene blue as a rescue.

333
00:31:27.839 --> 00:31:33.900
<v Tutor>And rhythm: atrial fibrillation and heart block, which is why epicardial wires are left in place.

334
00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:41.256
<v Tutor>Then there is the trap from the 2025 first sitting, question twelve: systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve.

335
00:31:41.256 --> 00:31:48.496
<v Tutor>You scored seven out of ten on it on 12 October, and the points that would have taken it higher were in the definition.

336
00:31:48.496 --> 00:31:49.978
<v Tutor>Here's the recall.

337
00:31:49.978 --> 00:31:54.026
<v Tutor>Systolic anterior motion causes two physiological consequences.

338
00:31:54.026 --> 00:31:55.338
<v Tutor>Name them both.

339
00:31:55.338 --> 00:31:56.820
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

340
00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:09.359
<v Tutor>First, dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, giving low output despite a hypercontractile ventricle.

341
00:32:09.359 --> 00:32:14.801
<v Tutor>Second, mitral regurgitation, usually directed posteriorly, causing pulmonary oedema.

342
00:32:14.801 --> 00:32:19.014
<v Tutor>The examiners said the higher-scoring definitions included both.

343
00:32:19.014 --> 00:32:32.940
<v Tutor>The management follows the physiology: fluid to fill the ventricle, slow the heart and keep sinus rhythm, wean inotropes, raise afterload with a pure vasoconstrictor, and return to theatre if it's refractory after a mitral repair.

344
00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:37.946
<v Tutor>Second, heart failure and cardiogenic shock, from Oh's chapter 33.

345
00:32:37.946 --> 00:32:47.157
<v Tutor>The examiner wants a definition, the Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions stages from A to E, and a structured approach.

346
00:32:47.157 --> 00:32:50.331
<v Tutor>Treat the cause, usually revascularisation.

347
00:32:50.331 --> 00:32:59.169
<v Tutor>Support the pressure with noradrenaline first, then add an inodilator such as dobutamine, milrinone or levosimendan to support output.

348
00:32:59.169 --> 00:33:03.526
<v Tutor>Then mechanical support, and here the trials are worth naming.

349
00:33:03.526 --> 00:33:14.293
<v Tutor>IABP-SHOCK II in 2012: 600 patients with infarct-related shock, no difference in 30-day mortality, 39.7 versus 41.3 percent, so routine balloon pumps were abandoned.

350
00:33:14.293 --> 00:33:31.471
<v Tutor>Don't forget the simpler therapies for acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema: sit the patient up, give non-invasive ventilation to reduce preload and afterload and the work of breathing, glyceryl trinitrate if the blood pressure allows, and loop diuretics for congestion.

351
00:33:31.471 --> 00:33:41.740
<v Tutor>ECLS-SHOCK in 2023: 420 patients, venoarterial ECMO did not reduce 30-day mortality, 47.8 versus 49.0 percent, with more bleeding and vascular complications.

352
00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:58.085
<v Tutor>DanGer Shock in 2024: 360 patients with ST elevation infarction and shock, a microaxial flow pump reduced six-month mortality from 58.5 to 45.8 percent, at the cost of more limb ischaemia, bleeding and kidney replacement therapy.

353
00:33:58.085 --> 00:34:04.360
<v Tutor>So, beyond this week: selected patients, experienced centres, and a clear exit plan.

354
00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:09.725
<v Tutor>Third, acute coronary syndromes and ischaemic heart disease, Oh's chapter 29.

355
00:34:09.725 --> 00:34:13.698
<v Tutor>The examiner wants reperfusion timing and the complications.

356
00:34:13.698 --> 00:34:23.862
<v Tutor>ST elevation infarction: primary PCI if it can happen within 120 minutes of diagnosis; otherwise fibrinolysis, ideally within ten minutes of diagnosis, then transfer.

357
00:34:23.862 --> 00:34:35.720
<v Tutor>In cardiogenic shock with multivessel disease, CULPRIT-SHOCK in 2017 showed that treating the culprit lesion only reduced death or kidney replacement therapy at 30 days, 45.9 versus 55.4 percent.

358
00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:49.156
<v Tutor>Then the mechanical complications that turn up as a new murmur and sudden shock in the first week: papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation, ventricular septal rupture, and free wall rupture with tamponade.

359
00:34:49.156 --> 00:34:55.640
<v Tutor>Echo makes the diagnosis; surgery is usually the answer; a balloon pump or other support is the bridge.

360
00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:59.799
<v Tutor>Fourth, aortic dissection and aneurysm, Oh's chapter 35.

361
00:34:59.799 --> 00:35:08.844
<v Tutor>Stanford type A involves the ascending aorta and needs emergency surgery; untreated, early mortality rises by roughly one to two percent per hour.

362
00:35:08.844 --> 00:35:18.299
<v Tutor>Type B is managed medically unless it's complicated by malperfusion, rupture or uncontrolled pain, in which case endovascular repair is the usual answer.

363
00:35:18.299 --> 00:35:25.700
<v Tutor>The medical target is impulse control: heart rate below 60 and systolic pressure of 100 to 120 millimetres of mercury.

364
00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.227
<v Tutor>The order matters.

365
00:35:27.227 --> 00:35:37.035
<v Tutor>Beta-blocker first, usually esmolol or labetalol, then a vasodilator, so that you don't cause a reflex tachycardia that increases the shear on the aortic wall.

366
00:35:37.035 --> 00:35:44.200
<v Tutor>CT angiography is the usual diagnostic test, and transoesophageal echo is the alternative in the unstable patient.

367
00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:49.374
<v Tutor>Fifth, hypertensive crisis and the antihypertensives, from Oh's chapter 27.

368
00:35:49.374 --> 00:36:00.072
<v Tutor>The key distinction is emergency versus urgency: the emergency has acute end-organ damage, such as encephalopathy, pulmonary oedema, dissection, acute kidney injury or eclampsia.

369
00:36:00.072 --> 00:36:01.567
<v Tutor>Here's the recall.

370
00:36:01.567 --> 00:36:08.585
<v Tutor>In a hypertensive emergency without dissection, how far do you lower the mean arterial pressure in the first hour?

371
00:36:08.585 --> 00:36:10.080
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

372
00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.978
<v Tutor>By no more than 25 percent in the first hour.

373
00:36:18.978 --> 00:36:26.105
<v Tutor>Then towards 160 over 100 to 110 over the next two to six hours, then to normal over 24 to 48 hours.

374
00:36:26.105 --> 00:36:33.562
<v Tutor>Autoregulation has shifted to the right, so a rapid fall can cause cerebral, coronary or renal ischaemia.

375
00:36:33.562 --> 00:36:53.888
<v Tutor>The exceptions are the examiners' favourites: aortic dissection, where you go fast and low; severe pre-eclampsia, where you treat a systolic of 160 or more promptly; and acute ischaemic stroke, where you lower only to below 185 over 110 before thrombolysis, and keep it below 180 over 105 afterwards.

376
00:36:53.888 --> 00:37:09.000
<v Tutor>Name your drugs with a reason: glyceryl trinitrate for pulmonary oedema, labetalol or hydralazine in pregnancy, esmolol in dissection, and sodium nitroprusside as a potent titratable option, watching for cyanide toxicity.

377
00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:12.830
<v Tutor>Sixth, valvular heart disease, from Oh's chapter 34.

378
00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:15.974
<v Tutor>The examiner wants the haemodynamic principles.

379
00:37:15.974 --> 00:37:25.749
<v Tutor>Severe aortic stenosis: a valve area under one square centimetre, a mean gradient above 40 millimetres of mercury, or a peak velocity above four metres per second.

380
00:37:25.749 --> 00:37:39.182
<v Tutor>The ventricle is stiff and preload-dependent, so keep sinus rhythm, avoid tachycardia, and treat hypotension early with a vasoconstrictor, because a fall in diastolic pressure starves a hypertrophied ventricle of coronary flow.

381
00:37:39.182 --> 00:37:44.956
<v Tutor>Acute mitral regurgitation: reduce afterload, consider a balloon pump, and get surgical help.

382
00:37:44.956 --> 00:37:50.958
<v Tutor>Acute aortic regurgitation: avoid bradycardia, never use a balloon pump, and plan urgent surgery.

383
00:37:50.958 --> 00:38:06.620
<v Tutor>Infective endocarditis belongs here too: the indications for early surgery are heart failure from valve destruction, uncontrolled infection such as an abscess or a resistant organism, and prevention of embolism from a large vegetation, usually above ten millimetres.

384
00:38:06.620 --> 00:38:09.993
<v Tutor>In each case, say what the ventricle needs and why.

385
00:38:09.993 --> 00:38:13.480
<v Tutor>That rationale is what turns a pass into a good mark.

386
00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.419
<v Tutor>Before the next episode, do one thing.

387
00:38:16.419 --> 00:38:28.449
<v Tutor>Take a blank page and write the neuroprognostication answer from memory as a two-column table: test on the left, limitation on the right, with confounders, timing and the two-or-more rule as your opening lines.

388
00:38:28.449 --> 00:38:30.325
<v Tutor>Give yourself ten minutes.

389
00:38:30.325 --> 00:38:36.782
<v Tutor>Then check it against Oh's chapter 30, the post-resuscitation section from page 1002, and fix what's missing.

390
00:38:36.782 --> 00:38:42.080
<v Tutor>Keep this week's daily ECG drills going: abnormality, cause and consequence, every time.
